[{:seon.task/name "the-branch-roster-is-compacted"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the branch roster is a konserve append log (`datahike.roster`), one entry a `branch!` or delete, never compacted. ``d/branches`` reads the whole log, one konserve read an entry (11.7k ticks and 33 KB each, measured 2026-11-11), deleted branches included: 94k ticks at 1 branch, 1.25 M at 100, 11.8 M and 34 MB at 1,000. A root's store gains a branch an agent, so the log only grows. Correct: the log is folded into one value when it is read past a bound, or a delete rewrites it, so ``d/branches`` costs the live branches (one read when compact). Upstream's own versioning first (`branch-history`, durable GC roots): take its shape if it has one. Proof when built: ``d/branches`` after n branches made and deleted costs what the live ones cost. Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.db/a-long-running-store-stays-bounded (A user's store runs for months with no one erasing it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.versioning-test/the-branch-roster-is-compacted}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-takes-a-branchs-facts-unchanged"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps (root's order 2026-11-16: deps leads; data consults and owns seon.db/an-id-is-made-where-its-entity-is-made, an input). The owner: a branch's work merges into main as it is, nothing rewritten (the same eids, each branch transaction's identity kept); merges make sense and cause no conflicts, a needed constraint found and stated; RESEARCH FIRST, the best answer not a fast one. His test case: a message written on main, seen in a branch by a merge down, then the branch merged up: one message in one consistent history, or a duplicate accepted knowingly. STATUS: research in progress; no plan yet. FINDINGS SO FAR [M unless marked]: (1) Seon already carries a branch's datoms with their original tx ids into main (seon.merge/carried-tx, merge.clj:503-532; merge-main adds head's datoms and their tx entities, :665-670; Datahike's carried-tx check, db/transaction.cljc:908-911, needs every carried tx at or below this transaction's own id). (2) So main's tx order is already not its commit order: of main's last 2,000 commits on the standing store, 219 hold a tx above a newer commit's lowest tx, 2,826 datoms [root result/bb84ef2c]; and none of the 2,000 is a two-parent commit: a merge into main is recorded single-parent, so the commit graph does not know a branch was merged. (3) What reads tx numbers as apply order, and is wrong after any merge today (survey, file:line in deps' notes of 2026-11-16 below): as-of/since (db.cljc:144-153), the history fold (db.cljc:195-212; seon problems.clj:304, merge.clj:523, union.clj:65), the inbox's since (server.clj:468, system.clj:2164,2183: a message merged below a since already returned is never delivered), commit-tx-range/point-base/walk-to-tx/commit-holding-tx (versioning.cljc:673-729,790-824), fork-database's max-tx walk (:1080-1090), seon ctx/wire.clj:83-88. Clean of tx numbers: merge-base (by generation), d/changes (by :base, fixed in Datahike deda581a), force-branch!. (4) Id partition (datahike/constants.cljc:5-14: e < tx0=2^29 <= tx <= txmax=2^40-1 < ce0=2^40 <= content <= emax=2^60-1) and its readers: max-eid kept below tx0 (db.cljc:1068-1076, db/transaction.cljc:110,561,682), next-eid's exhaustion at tx0 (:89), content-eid's range (:67-68), entity-id's check (:1087), 'a number >= tx0 is a tx' (api/impl.cljc:180, query.cljc:2763), every slice's sentinels (40+ sites, need only the constants), whole? in index/persistent_set.cljc:319-321 (every id within emax), the planner using max-eid as the population (query.cljc:3716, query/estimate.cljc:319-320, query/plan.cljc:1234-1236: time ids would make it the clock). (5) Width: leaves store Datom fields as zigzag varints (konserve jfasl.ss:12,83-92): a tx near 2^59 costs 9 bytes against 5 today, +4 B a datom; ids change every leaf's bytes and address, so the change is an erase and re-import, the owner's act. (6) The constraint that makes merges conflict-free already exists as a rule, not a rail: docs/sharing.md#where-a-fact-lives, each attribute of an entity is set on one branch; messages and root's observations live on main only and no merge carries them. OPEN, to research next: the field (Datomic's partitions and squuids, XTDB's bitemporality, Dolt's cell-wise merge over a commit DAG, git, HLC, CRDT ids); whether a fact's tx (when it was made) and main's knowledge of it (its commit) should be two dimensions, with as-of by commit; the measured cost of each option on the standing store."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-message-merged-down-then-up-is-one-message-in-one-order}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.merge{merge-main {:n 2000, :order :linear, :ticks 90000000, :bytes 300000000}}
